  "summary": "I reconciled the outpatient medication lists for all four patients against their St. Brigid discharge documents and pharmacy fill histories, following RBC-TOC-02. Each patient now has a signed progress note. Six items need your decision: one task and five messages to you.\n\n**Walter Kim (pt-0005)**\n- **Furosemide:** changed from 20 mg daily to 40 mg twice daily (new order mr-t0001). The discharge summary said 40 mg daily, but the hospital's signed correction to it says twice daily, and the correction takes priority.\n- **Potassium chloride:** stopped (mr-00076), as the discharge summary instructs.\n- **Omeprazole:** kept. Pantoprazole was only a hospital formulary substitute, so I did not order it.\n- **Metoprolol XL, atorvastatin, aspirin:** unchanged.\n- **Lisinopril — needs you:** still on hold. The hospital said to resume it once creatinine is below 1.5. The 10/21 outside lab shows creatinine 1.32, but potassium is 5.4, and policy forbids restarting an ACE inhibitor when potassium is above 5.0. I created an urgent task for you (task-t0003, due 10/26) to repeat potassium and decide on lisinopril.\n\n**Terence Stark (pt-0004)**\n- **Carvedilol (Coreg):** increased from 6.25 mg to 12.5 mg twice daily (new order mr-t0005).\n- **Aspirin and insulin 70/30 18 units twice daily:** unchanged.\n- **Atorvastatin 80 mg — messaged you:** no change made. The discharge summary continues it, but the last pharmacy fill was 3/4/2026. That covers only about 24% of the 180 days before admission, below the 80% threshold that counts as taking it as prescribed. His LDL was 134 on 10/8.\n- **Glipizide 5 mg — messaged you:** filled at an outside pharmacy on an endocrinologist's prescription, with no clinic order. The discharge summary doesn't mention it, so I left it alone. It's worth checking whether he should still take it alongside insulin, given his kidney disease.\n\n**Adelaide Thompson (pt-0042)**\n- **Nifedipine ER (Procardia XL):** increased from 30 mg to 60 mg daily (new order mr-t0009).\n- **Hydrochlorothiazide:** stopped (mr-00362). The hospital's signed correction to the summary says stop and do not restart, because it caused her low sodium.\n- **Levothyroxine 100 mcg — messaged you:** no change made. Fills cover only about 41% of the 180 days before admission. Her hospital TSH was 7.9.\n\n**Earnest Roberts (pt-0044)**\n- **Lisinopril 20 mg:** resumed from hold. The hospital's condition was creatinine below 1.3, and the 10/22 outside lab shows 1.08. That lab's potassium is 4.6; it is more recent than the 5.3 from 10/20, so the potassium rule doesn't block it.\n- **Rosuvastatin:** kept. Atorvastatin was only a hospital formulary substitute, so I did not order it.\n- **Both insulins:** unchanged.\n- **Sertraline 100 mg — messaged you:** filled at an outside pharmacy on a behavioral health prescription, with no clinic order. The discharge summary doesn't address it, so I left it alone.\n\nI left the other open items in your inbox untouched; none of them involve these four patients.",
